"""English speaking notes based on the user's current, edited poster."""
from pathlib import Path
import sys, hashlib, subprocess, tempfile, html
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
sys.path.insert(0,str(ROOT/'scratch/poster_deps'))
from pptx import Presentation
OUT=ROOT/'supervisor_package/poster'
poster=OUT/'Ruposhi_Bytes_Project_Poster.pptx'
deck=Presentation(poster)
texts=[s.text.strip() for s in deck.slides[0].shapes if s.has_text_frame and s.text.strip()]
title=next(t for t in texts if t.startswith('Deep Reinforcement Learning-Based'))
digest=hashlib.sha256(poster.read_bytes()).hexdigest()
def page(n,heading,body):
    return f'<section><div class="eyebrow">RUPOSHI BYTES · POSTER PRESENTATION GUIDE · {n} / 8</div><h1>{heading}</h1>{body}</section>'
def math(body):return '<div class="equation"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">'+body+'</math></div>'
ch=math('<msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>uv</mi><mi>eff</mi></msubsup><mo>(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>uv</mi><mi>direct</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>uv</mi><mi>RIS</mi></msubsup><mo>(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>)</mo>')
act=math('<msub><mi mathvariant="bold">a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mover><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>]</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1em"/><mo>|</mo><mi mathvariant="script">A</mi><mo>|</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>5</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3125</mn>')
reward=math('<msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3.5</mn><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.4</mn><mi>E</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo>')
ci=math('<mover><mi>Δ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>±</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>0.975</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mfrac><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Δ</mi></msub><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt></mfrac>')
pages=[]
pages.append(page(1,'Start with the story',f'''<p class="title">{html.escape(title)}</p><p>Team: Ruposhi Bytes · Department of Computer Science &amp; Engineering<br>Guide: Dr. Rima Deka, Assistant Professor, Department of Electronics<br>Assam down town University · 14 November 2026</p><div class="say"><strong>A natural opening — about one minute</strong><p>“Good morning. We are Ruposhi Bytes. Our project studies physical-layer security in optical wireless communication. We use an indoor visible-light system, where Alice sends confidential information to Bob through LED light. Another receiver, Eve, may also receive that light.</p><p>Friendly jammers can disturb Eve, but their signals may also interfere with Bob. We therefore added an optical reconfigurable intelligent surface, or RIS: a set of six controllable mirrors. Our controller selects both the jammer weights and a mirror configuration.</p><p>We built a Python simulation, connected it to an interactive 3D demo, and compared three schemes using ten independent seeds. Fixed RIS improved secrecy in our nominal experiment. Joint DRL control is implemented, but its extra benefit is not yet statistically established.”</p></div><h2>What the title means</h2><p><strong>OWC</strong> is optical wireless communication. <strong>VLC</strong>, using visible LED light, is the specific OWC system implemented here. Do not suggest that the project evaluates every type of optical wireless link.</p><p><strong>Physical-layer security</strong> studies confidential communication through the properties of the communication channels. Our simulation reports secrecy-rate and receiver-quality metrics. It does not demonstrate an encrypted application or actual stolen files.</p><h2>How to use these notes</h2><p>Use pages 1–2 as your speaking route. Pages 3–5 explain the equations and results. Pages 6–8 cover the demo, questions and handover between members. Speak in your own words; you do not need to read the entire document aloud.</p>'''))
pages.append(page(2,'Walk through the poster', '''<h2>1 · Abstract and keywords</h2><p>“The abstract brings together our problem, our RIS extension, the controller and the evaluation. The main point is that we tested both the expected benefit and the cases where secrecy fails.”</p><p>Do not read the abstract word for word. Explain the problem first, then point to the architecture and results.</p><h2>2 · Problem statement and motivation</h2><p>“LED transmission is a broadcast within the illuminated region. A second receiver may collect the signal. We want to reduce Eve’s useful reception without damaging Bob’s link too much.”</p><p>A passive Eve does not automatically create interference at Bob. The trade-off comes from friendly jamming and the geometry. Human-body blockage or an active attacker would require another declared model.</p><h2>3 · Objectives and scope</h2><p>“Our objectives were to add finite RIS reflections to the baseline, implement joint control, compare schemes fairly and record both good and adverse results.”</p><p>The scope is a static 5 × 5 × 3 m room with known Eve geometry. This is an initial simulation study, not a mobile deployment.</p><h2>4 · Methodology and system design</h2><p>Follow the diagram from Alice and the four jammers to Bob, Eve and the RIS. Then point to the controller: “The mirrors change the channels. The controller selects the jammer weights and mirror configuration, receives a reward, and updates its action-value estimates during training.”</p><h2>5 · Implementation</h2><p>“Python calculates the optical channels and metrics. PyTorch runs the CNN/DND learner. The local browser displays the geometry, controls and recorded results. The animated pulses are illustrations; they are not packet measurements.”</p><h2>6–9 · Results, application, conclusion and references</h2><p>Explain the three schemes and the confidence intervals using page 5. The application is a simulation study for indoor optical security. Finish with the measured result, the limits and the next experiment—not a claim that every attacker is defeated. The references identify the 2024 baseline and the episodic-control background.</p>'''))
pages.append(page(3,'Explain the equations on the poster',ch+'''<p><strong>Read it:</strong> “The effective channel from a transmitter to a receiver equals the direct channel plus the reflected RIS contribution for configuration q.”</p><p><em>h</em> is channel gain: how strongly a transmitted signal reaches a receiver. The subscripts <em>u, v</em> identify the endpoints; for example, <em>AB</em> means Alice to Bob. The superscripts name the contribution. <em>q</em> is the mirror codebook choice.</p><p>In one nominal manual configuration, the direct Alice–Bob gain is 2.71827 × 10⁻⁵ and the reflected gain is 6.35546 × 10⁻⁶. Their sum is 3.35381 × 10⁻⁵, approximately 23.4% above the direct gain. This is a <em>channel-gain</em> change, not a 23.4% secrecy-rate claim.</p>'''+act+'''<p><strong>Read it:</strong> “One action contains four jammer weights and a normalized RIS configuration index. There are 3,125 available joint actions.”</p><p>Each jammer has five discrete weight choices: −1, −0.5, 0, 0.5 and 1. Four jammers give 5⁴ = 625 vectors. Five RIS configurations give 625 × 5 = 3,125 choices. This is the number of actions, not a 3,125-dimensional vector.</p><p>The last coordinate, q with a tilde, encodes the codebook index in [−1, 1]. For this five-state codebook, −1, −0.5, 0, 0.5 and 1 encode q = 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4. It is not a physical mirror angle. Negative jammer weights reverse the modulation waveform around the DC bias; they do not mean negative optical power.</p>'''+reward+'''<p><strong>Read it:</strong> “The reward increases with secrecy rate and decreases with Bob’s error probability and jammer cost.”</p><p><em>cₛ</em> is secrecy rate; <em>pₑ</em> is Bob’s bit-error probability; <em>E</em> is the model-defined jammer cost. The coefficients 3.5 and 0.4 determine the penalties. Higher reward does not mean that every individual metric is best.</p><p>The primary study uses nonnegative L1 cost. The printed paper’s signed-cost interpretation is different. This utility has no hard service guarantee, and the primary run does not activate the optional extra QoS penalty. RIS actuator energy is not included.</p>'''))
pages.append(page(4,'How the learning and optical model work', '''<h2>What the mirrors actually do</h2><p>The finite specular model uses the source position, each mirror’s center and normal, its size and reflectivity, and the receiver geometry. It applies visibility and field-of-view checks and bounds collected power by reflected intercepted power. All source–receiver links use the same physical mirror normals for a selected configuration.</p><p>We cannot independently choose a good Alice–Bob reflection and an unrelated jammer–Eve reflection from the same mirror at the same instant. Some desired paths remain unavailable. A configuration name describes its aiming intention, not a guaranteed gain.</p><h2>The five mirror patterns</h2><table><tr><th>q</th><th>Purpose</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>Inactive/stowed zero-reflection reference.</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Aim the tiles from Alice toward Bob.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Assign tiles to aim jammer light toward Eve.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Split tiles between the Bob and Eve targets.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Use nominal neutral mirror normals.</td></tr></table><h2>What CNN and DND mean</h2><p>The <strong>CNN</strong> converts a padded history of states and actions into a feature key. The current state contains previous Bob BER, previous secrecy rate, the installed effective Alice–Bob gain and previous cost. The history window is 11 slots. In the matched study it is padded to an 11 × 11 input and processed by two Conv2d layers and fully connected layers.</p><p>The <strong>DND</strong>, or Differentiable Neural Dictionary, keeps feature keys and learned action values for each discrete action. Similar stored keys contribute through Gaussian kernel weights. The controller uses those value estimates to choose actions.</p><h2>Training versus evaluation</h2><p>During 2,000 training slots, the agent explores, updates its dictionary and trains the CNN. During the separate 1,000-slot greedy evaluation, exploration and learning updates are disabled. The environment starts a fresh episode. Each of the ten seeds trains a separate agent.</p><p>The larger joint action space may need more samples; that is a hypothesis to investigate, not a proven explanation for every observed difference. We have not introduced a new actor–critic algorithm or demonstrated online learning against a real unknown Eve.</p>'''))
pages.append(page(5,'Explain the results without overstating them', '''<table><tr><th>Scheme</th><th>What is controlled?</th><th>Mean secrecy<br>bps/Hz</th></tr><tr><td>No RIS</td><td>Jammer weights; reflection disabled.</td><td>1.3205</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed RIS</td><td>Jammer weights; q = 1 fixed.</td><td>1.4978</td></tr><tr><td>Joint RIS</td><td>Jammer weights and q.</td><td>1.4565</td></tr></table><p><strong>Say:</strong> “These are nominal L1 frozen-evaluation results. Each dot is one seed’s mean, the diamond is the group mean, and the bars are 95% confidence intervals across seeds.”</p><h2>Why the paired result matters</h2><p>We subtract two schemes’ scores within each matching seed, then estimate the mean difference and its uncertainty. Do not judge a comparison only by whether the separate group bars overlap.</p>'''+ci+'''<p>Δ is a same-seed difference, the bar denotes its mean, sΔ is its sample standard deviation, and N = 10. The t multiplier accounts for uncertainty with a small seed sample.</p><table><tr><th>Comparison</th><th>Mean difference</th><th>Paired 95% CI</th></tr><tr><td>Fixed − baseline</td><td>+0.1773</td><td>[+0.0378, +0.3167]</td></tr><tr><td>Joint − baseline</td><td>+0.1359</td><td>[−0.0440, +0.3158]</td></tr><tr><td>Joint − fixed</td><td>−0.0413</td><td>[−0.2674, +0.1847]</td></tr></table><p><strong>Say:</strong> “Fixed RIS has a positive paired interval, supporting a benefit in this setting. Joint RIS has a higher mean than baseline, but its interval includes zero. We therefore do not claim joint superiority. We also cannot claim that joint and fixed are equivalent.”</p><p>The fixed-RIS mean is about 13.4% above baseline. This percentage concerns secrecy rate in this experiment; it is different from the manual 23.4% channel-gain example.</p><p>All three schemes have zero numerically modeled evaluation outages here. Each static evaluation run repeats one selected action, so its 1,000 slots are not 1,000 independent channel conditions. Training outages are separate and must not be presented as evaluation advantages.</p>'''))
pages.append(page(6,'Run a clear five-minute demonstration', '''<h2>Before the meeting</h2><p>From the project folder, run <code>python launch_supervisor_demo.py</code> or double-click <code>run_supervisor_demo.bat</code>. Use the printed URL ending in <code>/demo</code>; the port can change. Keep the terminal open. Refresh with Ctrl+F5 after code updates.</p><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>What to do and say</th></tr><tr><td>1 · Locate devices</td><td>Show Alice on the ceiling, four jammers, Bob, Eve and six mirrors. Rotate the camera. “This view uses backend coordinates and mirror normals.”</td></tr><tr><td>2 · Manual off/on</td><td>Choose Nominal, benchmark weights and L1. Compare q0 with q1. “Everything else is unchanged; the reflected channel is the added contribution.”</td></tr><tr><td>3 · Read metrics</td><td>Show direct, reflected and effective gains, secrecy rate and Bob BER. Explain that a stronger Bob channel does not alone prove secrecy; Eve also matters.</td></tr><tr><td>4 · Show evidence</td><td>Go to the saved ten-seed frozen results. Explain fixed-RIS benefit and joint uncertainty. These saved results remain nominal even if manual positions change.</td></tr><tr><td>5 · Execute DRL</td><td>Run a 24-slot preview. Show selected weights and q, then recorded-action replay. “This proves execution, not trained-policy superiority.”</td></tr><tr><td>6 · Show a limit</td><td>Select near-Bob or colocated. “The tested near-Bob finite action grid has zero secrecy. We do not hide that case.”</td></tr></table><h2>Explain the animation correctly</h2><p>Moving pulses illustrate link activity and direction. They do not measure photons, packet delivery, bit decoding or physical propagation time. Mirror transitions are visual interpolation. Aggregate reflected lines routed through the panel are schematic, not exact per-tile ray intersections.</p><p>Playback uses a recorded training sequence. It is not live retraining at every displayed frame. Keep the recorded run’s receiver positions and cost interpretation with its actions; do not compare its numbers with another manual scenario.</p><h2>If something fails</h2><p>Do not guess a result. Use the saved poster graph or local screenshot, explain the setup and continue. If the server stops, restart the launcher and use its new URL. Stop the service with Ctrl+C after the demonstration.</p>'''))
pages.append(page(7,'Questions you are likely to hear', '''<h2>“What did your team add to the 2024 model?”</h2><p>“We added a finite optical RIS channel model and joint RIS/jammer actions to the existing VLC/FJ learning framework, then built a backend-linked demonstration and matched evaluation.”</p><h2>“Why is this called an intelligent surface?”</h2><p>“The mirror orientations are controllable. Our controller selects among a small set of configurations to alter the communication environment. The mirrors themselves do not decode messages.”</p><h2>“Where is the security if Eve still receives light?”</h2><p>“Receiving optical power is not the same as recovering confidential information. We evaluate secrecy rate under the stated wiretap model, along with Bob’s receiver quality. We do not claim zero photons at Eve.”</p><h2>“Is Bob BER = 0 proof of error-free hardware?”</h2><p>“No. It is the formula’s numerical output in this static simulation. It is not a measurement of every transmitted bit in hardware or a service guarantee.”</p><h2>“Why is joint DRL not the best scheme?”</h2><p>“In our ten-seed evaluation, fixed RIS has the larger mean secrecy. Joint control has more available actions, but this training budget does not establish its superiority. More degrees of freedom do not guarantee better learned performance.”</p><h2>“Does failure near Bob prove a universal distance limit?”</h2><p>“No. Exhaustive search gives zero secrecy over our declared 3,125-action grid at that specific near-Bob geometry. Other surfaces, actions or receiver conditions could behave differently. Exact colocated identical channels are a separate boundary.”</p><h2>“How do you know Eve’s position?”</h2><p>“We supply it as known simulation geometry. This is an oracle assumption. We have not built a passive-Eve detector; location uncertainty is future work.”</p><h2>“Did you reproduce the published 2024 curves?”</h2><p>“Not exactly. Some normalization and implementation choices are unresolved. We keep those limits documented and use the same declared settings across our matched baseline and RIS comparisons.”</p>'''))
pages.append(page(8,'Finish the discussion and share the work', '''<h2>“Is this experimentally validated?”</h2><p>“It is a software simulation with model, regression and integration checks. We have not built or calibrated a physical LED/RIS prototype. The latest independently recorded full run had 94 passing tests; later presentation edits require their own checks.”</p><h2>“Is the whole combination new?”</h2><p>“Optical RIS, secure VLC and DRL have prior literature. Our poster presents our implementation and comparative findings. We are still checking the precise research contribution rather than claiming the combination is first-ever.”</p><h2>“Does it save total energy?”</h2><p>“We report a model-defined jammer cost. RIS actuator, controller and other hardware energy are excluded, so we do not claim measured total-system savings.”</p><h2>Suggested four-person speaking split</h2><table><tr><th>Member</th><th>Suggested responsibility</th></tr><tr><td>MAHIDUL AHASAN</td><td>Opening, problem and objectives.</td></tr><tr><td>ATIKUR RAHMAN</td><td>System diagram, channels and equations.</td></tr><tr><td>MD AMIT HASAN ROBI</td><td>Implementation, learning and live demo.</td></tr><tr><td>MD SAMIUR RAHMAN TANIM</td><td>Results, limitations and questions.</td></tr></table><p>This is only a rehearsal suggestion. Change it to suit who knows each part best. One member can operate the laptop while another speaks.</p><div class="say"><strong>A short closing</strong><p>“Our implementation shows how a finite optical RIS can be combined with friendly jamming and a joint learning controller. Fixed RIS improves secrecy in the tested nominal study. Joint control and difficult receiver geometries still need work. We would value your feedback on the next experiment that would best test this approach.”</p></div><h2>Last check before presenting</h2><p>Remember the three means: <strong>1.3205, 1.4978 and 1.4565 bps/Hz</strong>. Know which scheme each belongs to. Keep channel gain, secrecy rate, reward and hardware power separate. If you do not know an answer, say which assumption or result you need to check.</p><p class="small">This guide was prepared from the user-edited poster, not the earlier title. Team department: Computer Science &amp; Engineering. Guide department: Electronics. The poster file was read without modification. No new research run was performed to write these notes.</p><p class="source">Source PPTX SHA-256:<br>'''+digest+'''</p>'''))
document='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Ruposhi Bytes — Poster Presentation Guide</title><style>@page{size:A4;margin:17mm 17mm 16mm}*{box-sizing:border-box}body{font:10.2pt/1.36 'Segoe UI',Arial,sans-serif;color:#20384a;margin:0}section{break-after:page}section:last-child{break-after:auto}.eyebrow{font-size:8.5pt;letter-spacing:1px;color:#087e8b;font-weight:700;border-bottom:2px solid #d7e8ef;padding-bottom:9px;margin-bottom:20px}h1{font-size:24pt;line-height:1.17;color:#18324e;margin:0 0 18px}h2{font-size:12.5pt;color:#125e80;margin:17px 0 6px}p{margin:8px 0}.title{font-size:15pt;line-height:1.35;font-weight:650}.say{background:#edf5fa;border-left:4px solid #087e8b;padding:12px 17px;margin:15px 0}.equation{background:#f1f6fa;padding:11px 4px;margin:15px 0 9px;break-inside:avoid}math{font-family:'Cambria Math',math;font-size:17pt}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:10pt;margin:12px 0}td,th{text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #cfdee8;padding:6px 8px;vertical-align:top}th{background:#e8f1f7}code{font:10pt Consolas,monospace}.small,.source{font-size:9pt;color:#607685}.source{overflow-wrap:anywhere}em{font-family:'Cambria Math',serif}</style>'''+''.join(pages)+'</html>'
html_path=OUT/'Ruposhi_Bytes_Poster_Presentation_Guide_EN.html'
pdf=OUT/'Ruposhi_Bytes_Poster_Presentation_Guide_EN.pdf'
html_path.write_text(document,encoding='utf-8')
